# Tech Stack

- SwiftUI and Swift
- Alamofire (Networking)
- DI Container - Factory (https://github.com/hmlongco/Factory)
- GRDB (SQLite ORM)
- Firebase SDK
- GoogleSignIn
- JWTDecode, SimpleKeychain
- PhoneNumberKit
- SwiftGenPlugin (Resources codegen)
- IdentifiedCollections
- FlowStacks (Coordinator-style navigation)
- WindowOverlay
- SwiftLint (static analysis)

# Important rules you HAVE TO FOLLOW:

- Always add debug logs & comments in the code for easier debug & readability. Use project logger, you can find one inside Utility package.
- Every time you choose to apply a rule(s), explicitly state the rule(s) in the output. You can abbreviate the rule description to a single word or phrase
- If README.md file is changed, you need to update export_open_source.sh script to fix rows deletion from readme file. Be sure that this works correctly.

# Swift specific rules

## 1. Development Process

- Follow SwiftLint rules. You can find them in .swiftlint.yml file.
- Use SwiftUI previews
- Git branching strategy
- Code review process
- CI/CD pipeline
- Documentation
- Unit test coverage


## 2. Code Structure

- Use Swift's latest features and protocol-oriented programming.
- Prefer value types (structs) over classes.
- Use MVVM architecture with SwiftUI.
- Do not use @State in the SwiftUI View for view model observation. Instead, use View Model. Apply this rule for MVVM modules.
- Use `@State` only for local state that is managed by the view itself.
- Structure: App/, Core/, Data/, Domain/, Presentation/, Navigation/, Services/, Resources/.
- Follow Apple's Human Interface Guidelines.

  
## 3. Naming
- camelCase for vars/funcs, PascalCase for types
- Verbs for methods (fetchData)
- Boolean: use is/has/should prefixes
- Clear, descriptive names following Apple style
- Use camel case for network model variables


## 4. Swift Best Practices

- Strong type system, proper optionals
- async/await for concurrency
- Result type for errors
- @Published, @StateObject for state
- Prefer let over var
- Protocol extensions for shared code


## 5. UI Development

- SwiftUI first, UIKit when needed
- SF Symbols for icons
- Support dark mode, dynamic type
- SafeArea and GeometryReader for layout
- Handle all screen sizes and orientations
- Implement proper keyboard handling
- Implement view action methods inside view extension, see neighbour modules for examples. To find modules see Whimo/Features folder.
- Use FlowStacks-based Coordinator (see `Whimo/Navigation/Coordinator`) with `Screen` definitions in `Whimo/Navigation/Screen.swift`.
- Navigation inside View uses injected navigator/coordinator.
- Navigation inside ViewModel uses app-wide navigation service via `AppState` when needed to pass data.


## 6. Performance

- Profile with Instruments
- Lazy load views and images
- Optimize network requests
- Background task handling
- Proper state management
- Memory management
- Optimize ForEach loops by using `Identifiable` protocol and `IdentifiedArrayOf` array type


## 7. Data & State

- GRDB for complex models
- UserDefaults for preferences
- Keychain for jwt tokens, etc. sensitive information
- Combine for reactive code
- Clean data flow architecture
- Proper dependency injection
- Handle state restoration
- AppState object for UI
- Network layer uses `convertToSnakeCase` encoding, do not use CodingKeys enum to build network models
- Use neighbour network targets as example to build other network targets


## 8. Security

- Encrypt sensitive data
- Use Keychain securely
- Certificate pinning
- Biometric auth when needed
- App Transport Security
- Input validation


## 9. Testing & Quality

- Never change anything in program business logic if you work with tests
- Never change anything in tests if you work on business logic
- XCTest for unit tests
- XCUITest for UI tests
- Test common user flows
- Performance testing
- Error scenarios
- Accessibility testing
- For logs use `log.debug` or `log.error` or `log.info` or `log.fault`. Logger implementation can found here: Packages/Utility/Sources/Utility/Logger/Log.swift


## 10. Essential Features

- Deep linking support
- Push notifications
- Background tasks
- Localization
- Error handling
- Analytics/logging
- If needed to implement mock on some logic and display alert like "Feature in development": 1 - use `@Inject(\.alertManager) private var alertManager` dependency, 2 - call `alertManager.show(feature: AlertManager.AlertModel.Features.InDevelopment.self) { _ in nil }`to display alert. Important: do not forgot to make import of `import class CommonUI.AlertManager`


## 11. App Store Guidelines

- Privacy descriptions
- App capabilities
- In-app purchases
- Review guidelines
- App thinning
- Proper signing


Follow Apple's documentation for detailed implementation guidance.  